  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 11,457 feet, <strong>Currant Mountain</strong> is a summit in Nevada. The Currant Mountain Trail covers 1.9 miles with about 971 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steady climb. It's rated Moderate overall.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>The climbing is spread fairly evenly from start to finish rather than stacked into one steep section. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 1.65, gaining roughly 900 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 6,880 feet, the route tops out near 7,851 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=38.88827,-115.48373\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">38.88827, -115.48373</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 190 miles north of Las Vegas, Nevada. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>The reliable snow-free window is roughly July through September. At this elevation, afternoon thunderstorms are a serious summer hazard \u2014 start early and plan to be off the upper mountain by midday. Outside that window expect snow travel and bring traction and navigation skills to match.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Moderate rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 1.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Mount Grafton, Troy Peak and North Schell Peak \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
